From a22fb52fe5c52613d0d76b303a705868a8dd8063 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 20:12:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 40/40] lib/streamaggr, app/vmagent: flush on the grid, renamed
 scrape_time_intervals

A hard kill of vmagent in the lab, kill -9 during traffic, lost up to two
minutes of usage each time: everything aggregated and not yet flushed. With
flush_on_shutdown, which ACG sets on every rule, the flushes kept the phase of
the process's start, and an interval that had ended waited up to another one
for its flush.

- Under sample_timestamps a rule now flushes on the grid of its own interval,
  whatever its flush_on_shutdown: an interval is written as soon as it ends,
  and a process killed outright takes the interval in progress with it, not
  two. A rule without it keeps the phase of its start, as before, its outputs
  stamped with the flush's time.
- The flush ticker keeps the monotonic clock, and the wall clock drifts from
  it and is corrected against it (by 1.1 s every 30 s on a lab node): a tick a
  moment before the boundary by the wall clock left the interval that had just
  ended to the next flush, an interval later. The flush waits for the wall
  clock to get to the boundary.
- sample_timestamps is renamed scrape_time_intervals, which says what it does:
  every sample goes to the interval it was scraped in. The flags are
  -remoteWrite.streamAggr.scrapeTimeIntervals and
  -streamAggr.scrapeTimeIntervals. The former names still work, with a warning;
  the two set to different values in a rule are refused.

Each has a test that fails on v1.126.1035-cluster and passes now. In the lab,
two hard kills during 13 minutes of traffic lost 3,227 of 12,778 requests
before and none of 12,877 now, the round in progress at each kill having been
flushed already.
